<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pura Kehen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pura Kehen is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple in Bangli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, and the state temple of the former Kingdom of Bangli. Built in terraces on a hillside, it is among the oldest temples on the island, with inscriptions that go back to the early Balinese kingdoms. Its courtyards include an eleven-tiered meru shrine and a great banyan tree holding a kulkul drum tower.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pura-kehen.jpg" alt="Pura Kehen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esme Vos · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pura_Kehen_temple_complex_in_Bali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pura Kehen stands at the foot of a wooded hill in Cempaga, about 2 kilometres north of the centre of Bangli. Bangli, whose name is said to come from bang giri, red forest or red mountain, was one of the nine kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, and its regency was founded by the Gelgel Kingdom of the Majapahit dynasty. The temple is named in three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> inscriptions, of the late 9th, early 11th and 13th centuries, each under a different name and each tying it to the village of Bangli.</p><p>In the earliest the Brahmans who kept it called it Hyang Api, god of fire. By the early 11th century it was Hyang Kehen, from the Balinese keren, flame, and served as the official temple where royal officials swore oaths before the fire god Agni, using a vessel wound with four serpents, the bejana sarpantaka. Those proven unfaithful were held to bring a curse on themselves and their descendants. The name Pura Kehen appears in the 13th-century inscription.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple rises from south to north over a hilly outcrop in three courtyards. Terraced steps with statues of figures from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> lead to the outer court, whose central gateway is a paduraksa, a form usually kept for the entrance to the inner sanctum. There stands a sacred banyan tree at least 400 years old; the people of Bangli believe that a broken branch foretells a death, a king's, a brahmin's or a commoner's according to the side on which it breaks. Pavilions hold the gamelans.</p><p>A candi bentar gateway leads to the middle court of shrines and offering preparation. The inner court holds the 11-tiered meru, dedicated to the temple's protecting god, and a padmasana lotus shrine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Porcelain plates set in its wall include scenes of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with a watermill and a four-horse coach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The feast of Pagerwesi is held every six months to honour Hyang Widhi and his manifestations, and the Ngusaba Dewa, or Karya Agung Bhatara Turun Kabeh, falls on Purnama Kalima in early November. Smaller ceremonies honour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> and other gods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pura Kehen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its state temple, <b>Pura Kehen</b>, is one of the oldest on the island.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bangli">Kingdom of Bangli</a></li></ul></section>
